  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Wednesday...\n\nBroad ridging will keep the region warm and mostly dry through\nSaturday. As splitting energy moves to the north and south of the\nregion, a dry cold frontal passage is expected to move through \nFriday evening. Ahead of this front, conditions are expected to \nbe breezy ahead of and with the frontal passage with gusts 25-35 \nmph expected across southeast MT. Hi- res models generally have \nisolated light showers for portions of south central and southeast\nMT Friday evening. With a little instability across southeast MT,\nan isolated thunderstorm or two is possible Friday evening. This \nwill also cool temperatures from the upper 70s and low 80s (today \nand Friday) to the 60s and low 70s this weekend through mid week. \nEnsembles then depict an additional system working into the area \nlate Sunday into Monday, but the better forcing looks to track \nsouth. Weak forcing is currently producing some showers across the\nregion. Currently, there is about a 25-50% chance for at least a \nquarter inch of precipitation Sunday-Monday with about a 50-70% \nchance for area mountains. With this, there is about a 20-40% \nchance for at least 2 inches of snow, generally above 8500 ft \n(including the Beartooth Pass) Monday night into Tuesday morning. \nTS",
